<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mutesa I of Buganda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1837–<a href="/years/1884">1884</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mutesa I was kabaka of Buganda from 1856 to 1884. He strengthened royal power over the chiefs and opened his court to Arab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> traders, adopting some Islamic practices. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morton-stanley">Henry Morton Stanley</a> publicised his interest in 1875, he received Protestant and Catholic missionaries, whose rivalry with Muslim converts at court led to civil war under his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwanga-ii">Mwanga II</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mutesa-i-of-buganda.jpg" alt="Mutesa I of Buganda" width="150" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mutesa_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Muteesa was born at the Batandabezaala Palace at Mulago, a son of Kabaka Ssuuna II and of Nabakyala Muganzirwazza, one of his father's 148 recorded wives. The historian MSM Kiwanuka called him an insignificant, obscure prince beside his brothers Prince Kajumba, his father's preferred heir, and Prince Kiyimba. On Ssuuna's death in October 1856 the chiefs, led by the Katikkiro Kayiira, chose Muteesa because they thought Kajumba would be hard to control. Rival princes were imprisoned on Kisimi Island, and after a plot to replace the new king with Kiyimba was foiled, the conspirators and both princes were executed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Muteesa fought wars to expand Buganda, guard its trade routes and defend it against Bunyoro Kitara, the Khedivate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, armed slave traders and Buvuma. Buganda traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, barkcloth and slaves for firearms, salt, cloth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> wire from the East African coast and Zanzibar, and control of the routes let him limit the firearms that reached Bunyoro. He enlarged the kingdom's fleet on Lake Nnalubaale and backed Ruyonga with troops in Bunyoro's succession war after the death of the Omukama Kamurasi.</p><p>The first Europeans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hanning-speke">John Hanning Speke</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-augustus-grant">James Augustus Grant</a>, arrived in 1862. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-george-gordon">Charles George Gordon</a>, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s Equatoria province, sent envoys to divert Buganda's ivory from Zanzibar to Egypt; Charles Chaillé-Long claimed that Muteesa signed an agreement making Buganda a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Egypt, which other historians dispute. Seeking a stronger ally, Muteesa had Stanley write an open letter inviting missionaries, published in the Daily Telegraph in November 1875. Missionaries of the Church Missionary Society arrived in 1877 and the White Fathers in 1879.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Muteesa died at Kasubi Nabulagala on 9 October 1884 and was the first kabaka buried at Kasubi. He is reported to have had 87 wives and 98 children, among them the later kabakas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwanga-ii">Mwanga II</a>, Kiweewa Nnyonyintono and Kalema. Ugandan historians judge him variously: some regard him as the most important king of Buganda for his vision and diplomacy, while others hold that he welcomed foreign influence that undermined Buganda's sovereignty. Muteesa I Royal University was opened in his name in 2007.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muteesa I of Buganda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its open court made it the gateway through which Islam and Christianity entered the interior of East Africa: <b>Mutesa I</b> received Arab traders and, after the journalist Henry Morton Stanley publicised his interest in 1875, Protestant and Catholic missionaries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-buganda">Kingdom of Buganda · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mulago, in what is now <a href="/countries/uganda">Uganda</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasubi-tombs">Kasubi Tombs</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/uganda">Uganda</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=0.35,32.58&amp;year=1867" aria-label="Mulago on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Mutesa I of Buganda"><image href="/maps/globes/g03000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5221 4970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5221 4970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=0.35,32.58&amp;year=1867">What was here in 1867? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
